From 4700dfa13a1dda931d523d565999a71aa9fb7c46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PRIME B360M-C port <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 02:45:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] mb/asus: Add PRIME B360M-C

The ASUS PRIME B360M-C is a micro-ATX board for Coffee Lake-S CPUs with
an Intel B360 (Cannon Point PCH-H), four DDR4 UDIMM slots, a Nuvoton
NCT6793D Super I/O, an Intel I219-V PHY, a Realtek ALC887 and an ASMedia
ASM1083 PCIe to PCI bridge.

The port is based on the vendor firmware (BIOS 3202):
- GPIOs: the board GPIO_INIT_CONFIG table of the vendor firmware,
  converted with util/intelp2m -p cnl-h. Like the vendor firmware, which
  applies it early in PEI, the table is applied in the bootblock.
- Super I/O: the bootblock repeats the multi-function pin setup of the
  vendor PEI module and enables UART A (COM1 header) for the console.
  The hardware monitor only gets its base address; fan control is left
  at the power-on defaults.
- HDA verbs and VBT are taken verbatim from the vendor firmware.
- Memory: SPD addresses 0x50-0x53 as in the FSP defaults used by the
  vendor firmware; RCOMP and DQ settings as on asrock/h370m-itx_ac.
  SMBIOS type 17 uses the slot names on the board (DIMM_A1 to DIMM_B2).
- PCIe: the CLKSRC to root port mapping is not known, so every
  reference clock runs free and CLKREQ# is not used.
- Display: DDI E drives the DP to VGA converter, so the board selects
  SOC_INTEL_GFX_ENABLE_DDI_E_BIFURCATION.
- Options: a CMOS table with debug_level, power_on_after_fail and
  me_state. With EDK2 and SMMSTORE, a CFR setup menu offers me_state and
  power_on_after_fail instead. me_state=Disable soft-disables the ME
  through HECI.

Not implemented: fan control, TPM.

TEST=Boots Linux with the EDK2 payload and the FSP GOP. One DIMM in
DIMM_B2, NVMe in M.2_1, I219-V, ALC887, USB and HDMI work. With the
CMOS option me_state=Disable the ME reports Soft Temporary Disable and
HECI is hidden.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
