@@ -0,0 +1,176 @@
+# ASUS PRIME B360M-C
+
+This page describes how to run coreboot on the [ASUS PRIME B360M-C].
+
+```{note}
+Tested with the EDK2 payload and the FSP GOP: boots Linux with one DIMM in
+DIMM_B2, NVMe in M.2_1, I219-V LAN, ALC887 audio, USB and HDMI. Everything else
+below is implemented but not yet tested.
+```
+
+## Technology
+
+```{eval-rst}
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| CPU         | Intel 8th/9th Gen Core, Pentium, Celeron (Coffee Lake-S, LGA1151) |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| DRAM        | 4 DDR4 UDIMM slots, 2 per channel                                 |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Chipset     | Intel B360 (Cannon Point PCH-H)                                   |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Super I/O   | Nuvoton NCT6793D                                                  |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| LAN         | Intel I219-V (PCH GbE)                                            |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Audio       | Realtek ALC887                                                    |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| PCI         | ASMedia ASM1083 PCIe to PCI bridge (one PCI slot)                 |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Coprocessor | Intel CSME 12 (consumer)                                          |
++-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
+```
+
+## Required proprietary blobs
+
+```{eval-rst}
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+| Binary file  | Apply                                    | Required |
++==============+==========================================+==========+
+| FSP-M, FSP-S | Intel FSP for Coffee Lake (3rdparty/fsp) | yes      |
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+| Microcode    | 3rdparty/intel-microcode                 | yes      |
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+| IFD          | Intel Flash Descriptor                   | yes      |
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+| ME           | Intel CSME firmware                      | yes      |
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+| GbE          | I219-V configuration and MAC address     | yes      |
++--------------+------------------------------------------+----------+
+```
+
+The IFD, ME and GbE regions can be extracted from a backup of the flash chip
+with `ifdtool --platform cnl -x`. The ASUS update capsule (`.CAP`) also
+contains a complete 16 MiB image after a 2 KiB header, but its GbE region holds
+the placeholder MAC address `88:88:88:88:87:88` rather than the board's own.
+
+Flash layout of the vendor descriptor:
+
+```{eval-rst}
++------------+---------------------+
+| Region     | Range               |
++============+=====================+
+| Descriptor | 0x000000 - 0x000fff |
++------------+---------------------+
+| GbE        | 0x001000 - 0x002fff |
++------------+---------------------+
+| ME         | 0x003000 - 0x2fffff |
++------------+---------------------+
+| BIOS       | 0x300000 - 0xffffff |
++------------+---------------------+
+```
+
+## Building coreboot
+
+```bash
+make distclean
+touch .config
+./util/scripts/config --enable VENDOR_ASUS --enable BOARD_ASUS_B360M_C
+make olddefconfig
+make
+```
+
+To produce a full image, additionally enable `HAVE_IFD_BIN`, `HAVE_ME_BIN` and
+`HAVE_GBE_BIN` and point them to the extracted regions. Select "Use the preset
+values to protect the regions" (`DO_NOT_TOUCH_DESCRIPTOR_REGION`) to keep the
+vendor flash region permissions.
+
+## Flashing coreboot
+
+```{eval-rst}
++-------------------+------------------------------------------------+
+| Type              | Value                                          |
++===================+================================================+
+| Size              | 16 MiB                                         |
++-------------------+------------------------------------------------+
+| Model             | descriptor lists Winbond W25Q128 and           |
+|                   | GigaDevice GD25Q128 (not verified on a board)  |
++-------------------+------------------------------------------------+
+| Internal flashing | BIOS region only (descriptor and ME region are |
+|                   | read-only for the host)                        |
++-------------------+------------------------------------------------+
+```
+
+Back up the whole chip with an external programmer before the first flash.
+Afterwards the BIOS region can be updated internally:
+
+```bash
+flashrom -p internal --ifd -i bios -w coreboot.rom
+```
+
+## Display
+
+The vendor VBT describes four outputs:
+
+```{eval-rst}
++-----+-----------------------------------+-----------------+
+| DDI | Connector                         | libgfxinit port |
++=====+===================================+=================+
+| B   | DisplayPort 1 (dual mode)         | DP1 / HDMI1     |
++-----+-----------------------------------+-----------------+
+| C   | DisplayPort 2 (dual mode)         | DP2 / HDMI2     |
++-----+-----------------------------------+-----------------+
+| D   | HDMI                              | HDMI3           |
++-----+-----------------------------------+-----------------+
+| E   | D-Sub, through a DP to VGA bridge | none            |
++-----+-----------------------------------+-----------------+
+```
+
+libgfxinit does not support the DP to VGA converter on DDI E on this
+generation (the same is documented for the ASRock H110M-DVS), so the D-Sub
+port only works with the FSP GOP. The board selects
+`SOC_INTEL_GFX_ENABLE_DDI_E_BIFURCATION` so that DDI E keeps its two lanes for
+the operating system's driver.
+
+## Super I/O
+
+The bootblock repeats the multi-function pin setup done by the vendor
+firmware's Super I/O PEI module and enables UART A at 0x3f8 for the console
+(COM1 header). The hardware monitor is left at its power-on defaults apart from
+its base address (0x290), so there is no fan control.
+
+## Intel ME
+
+The ME can be soft-disabled through HECI with the `me_state` option. coreboot
+sends the request on the next boot and performs a global reset. This was
+verified on hardware with the CMOS option: afterwards the ME reports the "Soft
+Temporary Disable" operation mode and HECI is hidden.
+
+With the EDK2 payload, the board provides a CFR setup menu and stores the options
+as UEFI variables in SMMSTORE. The option appears under Device Manager, Platform
+Setup Menu, as "Intel Management Engine".
+
+With other payloads it is a CMOS option:
+
+```bash
+nvramtool -w me_state=Disable
+```
+
+## Implemented, untested
+
+- coreboot console on the COM1 header (115200 8N1), POST codes on port 0x80
+- DDR4 in all four slots (SPD addresses 0x50-0x53)
+- iGPU via libgfxinit (HDMI, DP) or FSP GOP
+- SATA ports 1-6, both M.2 sockets, PCIe x16, both PCIe x1 slots, PCI via ASM1083
+- USB (over-current reporting not configured)
+- I219-V LAN
+- ALC887 audio
+- S3 suspend
+- COM2, LPT and PS/2 keyboard/mouse
+
+## Not implemented
+
+- Fan control (the fans run with the Super I/O power-on defaults)
+- TPM header
+- CLKREQ# based PCIe clock power management (all reference clocks run free)
+
+[ASUS PRIME B360M-C]: https://www.asus.com/supportonly/prime%20b360m-c/helpdesk_knowledge/
